node_modules/
.next/
*.tsbuildinfo
.env
.env.*
.DS_Store

# per-project outcome records and tuning (raw data, never committed)
.ursa/

# Trial outputs: real outcome records and their viewers, built from real
# sessions. Never committed, and until now nothing said so — the docs cite
# ursa-major/trial/task-001/ and no branch has ever carried it. The
# redaction gate is a backstop, not this one's: it looks for session UUIDs,
# home paths and project slugs, and a record built from pasted
# conversations (fixtures/mini uses ids like 01-claude) carries verbatim
# prompt text with none of those shapes.
ursa-major/trial/*/

# Run scratch, inside the working tree on purpose.
#
# Seat runs on the resident host share /tmp: it is mode 1777, TMPDIR is
# unset, and every seat runs as the same user, so a bare /tmp/<noun> name is
# a shared mutable path and not a private one. On 2026-10-26 HQ's
# distribution seat composed a pull request body at such a path, a sibling
# seat wrote the same filename in between, and `gh pr edit` published the
# sibling's body. Both writes succeeded and no exit status reported it.
#
# The real repair is a private scratch directory exported by whatever
# provisions a run, and it is filed as a ledger entry. Until that exists, a
# run has two safe options: `mktemp -d`, or a path under this directory.
# This entry is what makes the second one safe, because the in-tree
# workaround is otherwise one `git add -A` away from committing a pull
# request body, and the seats that have used it so far relied on
# remembering to delete the file by hand.
.scratch/
#
# The same idea spelled differently, because the rule above is one exact
# directory name and a run that has to remember a name does not remember
# that one. On 2026-10-26 an exo window shipped a ship-first placeholder
# at `.exo-window-52-scratch` in the repository root, 75 bytes of benign
# text, and it is in `main`'s history permanently. Nothing was disclosed
# and that is luck, not a control: the hazard this rule exists to blunt is
# that the in-tree workaround is one `git add -A` away from committing
# whatever the run happened to be composing. No tracked file in this
# repository has ever had `scratch` in its name, so these cost nothing.
*-scratch
*.scratch
.scratch-*
scratch/
#
# The third spelling, and this one is not a spelling at all, because the two
# above are a list of names and the list keeps losing. Measured on
# 2026-11-02 over every object in every ref: seventeen root-level
# dot-entries have been committed to this repository as run scratch, and the
# five patterns above cover five of them. The twelve they miss are
# `.engineer-run-2026-09-28`, `.engineer-run-scratch.md`,
# `.exo-run-2026-10-26.md`, `.exo-run-2026-10-26-w57.md`,
# `.exo-window-40-opening`, `.exo-window-75-opening`,
# `.exo-window-scratch.md`, `.pm-run-scratch.md`, `.run`,
# `.skill-run-15.md`, `.skill-run-16.md` and `.ursa-run/`, and the last of
# those was still tracked on `main` six days after the comment above was
# written. Every one of them was a run writing a ship-first placeholder at
# its own opening, which is the moment the run is holding its wake payload.
#
# Python bytecode. This repository had no Python outside `docs/design/` until
# the vendored secret-print guard landed on 2026-11-09, and running its test
# suite writes `tools/tests/__pycache__/`. `__pycache__` is not a dot-entry,
# so the inverted root rule below does not reach it, and this run committed a
# 53 KB `.pyc` once before noticing.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# So the rule is inverted. Every root-level dot-entry is ignored and the
# four this repository actually commits are named back in. That needs no
# prediction about the next name a run invents, and it costs nothing: in
# the whole history of this repository the only root-level entries that are
# not dot-entries are the twelve real ones, so the inversion has never had
# a false positive to have.
#
# Adding a legitimate root dot-entry means adding a negation here. That is
# the intended friction, and `tools/root-entries.mjs` is the gate that
# fails when the tree and this list disagree.
/.*
!/.gitignore
!/.gitattributes
!/.github/
!/.claude/

# `.claude/` is negated above so one tracked file can live there: the
# PreToolUse registration in `.claude/settings.json`, which is the whole
# reason the secret-print guard runs at all. The negation on its own
# un-ignores the entire directory, and the harness writes session
# transcripts, prompt history and local permission overrides into it. So
# the directory is re-ignored here and the one tracked file named back in,
# which is the same inverted rule this file argues for above, applied one
# level down.
/.claude/*
!/.claude/settings.json
